<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <!--
      Hardened runtime entitlements.

      node-pty spawns through its own `spawn-helper` executable, and every
      terminal pane then execs a user binary (their login shell, `claude`,
      `codex`). Under the hardened runtime, library validation and the default
      inheritance rules block exactly that, which surfaces as "posix_spawnp
      failed" for every pane — including a plain shell.

      allow-jit and allow-unsigned-executable-memory are what V8 needs.
      disable-library-validation is what lets a signed app exec a helper and
      user binaries it did not sign itself.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      STILL OPEN, and it needs somebody with a signing identity.

      allow-dyld-environment-variables lets DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES inject code
      into the signed app, which alongside disable-library-validation lowers
      the bar for a local attacker persisting inside it. Both audits flagged
      it, and nothing here appears to need it: an earlier version of the
      comment above credited "the two disable-* keys" when there has only ever
      been one, so this looks like it was grouped in with the entitlement that
      does the work.

      It stays until somebody can prove it is unnecessary, because the failure
      mode is every pane failing to spawn in a shipped build.

      What was tried, so nobody repeats it: package:mac:dir, then ad-hoc sign
      with --options runtime and these entitlements, then spawn a pty inside
      the signed binary through ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE. That reports
      "posix_spawnp failed" with this key AND without it — the control fails
      too, so the harness cannot tell the two apart. Ad-hoc signing has no team
      identifier, and the inheritance rules that let a hardened app exec its
      helper do not apply the same way without one. The check needs a real
      Developer ID; there are zero identities on the machine this was written
      on, and electron-builder skips signing entirely there.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      Two more used to live here: files.user-selected.read-write and
      network.client. Both are App Sandbox entitlements, and this app does not
      set com.apple.security.app-sandbox — so neither did anything, while their
      comments described a folder picker being scoped and network access being
      granted. That reads as protection the app has never had, which is worse
      than no entitlement at all, because the next reader believes it. Removed;
      no test needed, since an App Sandbox key outside the App Sandbox is inert
      by definition.
    -->
  </dict>
</plist>
